          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>The deep fascia of the forearm alone cannot create three separate compartments. </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>The deep fascia of the forearm alone cannot create three separate compartments. Which additional structure serves as the boundary that divides the forearm into its anterior, posterior, and lateral compartments?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lateral intermuscular septum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The lateral intermuscular septum is found in the arm, not the forearm. It separates the anterior and posterior compartments of the arm along with the medial intermuscular septum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Interosseous membrane</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The interosseous membrane is a tough fibrous sheet connecting the radius and ulna. Together with the deep fascia, it creates the three forearm compartments — anterior (flexors), posterior (extensors), and lateral.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Palmar aponeurosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The palmar aponeurosis is located in the palm, not the forearm. It binds the skin to deeper structures, preventing the palm from sliding during grip.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bicipital aponeurosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The bicipital aponeurosis is a fibrous expansion of the biceps tendon in the cubital fossa. It protects the brachial artery and median nerve but does not create forearm compartments.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The interosseous membrane connects the radius and ulna and serves as the additional boundary that, together with the deep fascia, creates the three forearm compartments."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The interosseous membrane is a key boundary in the forearm and is clinically significant in compartment syndrome, where swelling in a closed compartment compresses nerves and blood vessels."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
