          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>On a lateral elbow radiograph, the anterior humeral line is drawn along the ante</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>On a lateral elbow radiograph, the anterior humeral line is drawn along the anterior cortex of the humerus. In a normal elbow, through which part of the capitulum should this line pass?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior third</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>If the line passes through only the anterior third, the capitulum has been displaced posteriorly relative to the humeral shaft — a sign of a supracondylar fracture with posterior displacement.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Middle third</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In a normal elbow, the anterior humeral line passes through the middle third of the capitulum. Deviation from this alignment is a key radiological sign of a supracondylar fracture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior third</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>If the line passes through the posterior third, the capitulum is displaced anteriorly — an abnormal finding that warrants further investigation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It should not intersect the capitulum at all</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The anterior humeral line should intersect the capitulum in a normal elbow. Complete failure to intersect indicates significant bony malalignment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In a normal lateral elbow radiograph, the anterior humeral line passes through the middle third of the capitulum. Any deviation suggests a supracondylar fracture with displacement."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The anterior humeral line is a simple but powerful tool for detecting supracondylar fractures on lateral elbow radiographs, especially in children where the fracture line may be subtle."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
