          "label": "<p>In Allen's test, both wrist arteries are compressed, the patient clenches their </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In Allen's test, both wrist arteries are compressed, the patient clenches their fist until the hand blanches, and then the ulnar artery alone is released. What does flushing of the hand within 5–7 seconds confirm?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The radial artery is patent and dominant</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allen's test does not assess the radial artery. The radial artery remains compressed throughout the test. It evaluates whether the ulnar artery alone can perfuse the hand.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The ulnar artery provides adequate collateral supply to the hand</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Rapid flushing after releasing only the ulnar artery confirms that the ulnar supply, via the palmar arches, can adequately perfuse the hand. This makes radial artery cannulation safe because the hand will not become ischaemic if the radial artery is occluded.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The palmar arterial arches are absent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Flushing actually confirms the opposite — that the palmar arches are intact and functional, providing communication between the radial and ulnar arterial systems.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The median nerve is functioning normally</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allen's test assesses arterial blood supply, not nerve function. Median nerve integrity is tested by different clinical examinations.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Allen's test confirms that the ulnar artery provides adequate collateral supply to the hand via the palmar arches. This ensures safe radial artery cannulation without risk of hand ischaemia."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Allen's test is a safety check performed before radial artery cannulation. It confirms that the ulnar artery can independently perfuse the hand through the palmar arches."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
