          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During upper limb development, the zone of polarising activity (ZPA) produces Sonic Hedgehog protein to control anterior-to-posterior patterning. What congenital anomaly results from excess Sonic Hedgehog signalling?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Syndactyly (fused digits)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Syndactyly results from failure of apoptosis to separate the digital rays during week 7 — a distinct process from Sonic Hedgehog signalling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Amelia (complete limb absence)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Amelia results from failure of limb bud formation in week 4. It is not related to Sonic Hedgehog signalling from the ZPA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Polydactyly (extra digits)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The ZPA controls anterior-to-posterior patterning via Sonic Hedgehog protein. Excess signalling disrupts this patterning and leads to the formation of extra digits — polydactyly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Phocomelia (shortened limb segments)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Phocomelia involves partial limb absence and is linked to disruption of proximal-to-distal growth (driven by the AER), historically associated with thalidomide exposure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Excess Sonic Hedgehog from the ZPA causes polydactyly (extra digits). Each signalling centre governs a specific axis: the AER drives proximal-to-distal growth, and the ZPA controls anterior-to-posterior patterning."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Understanding which signalling centre controls which axis of development helps you predict the anomaly from the mechanism: AER drives proximal-distal, ZPA drives anterior-posterior, and Wnt/BMP drives dorsal-ventral."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
